# DG-485. The Windows workstation's system-level git config sets
# core.autocrlf=true while the repo's own (user and local) config says
# false. Without this file, that system setting is free to rewrite a
# worktree to CRLF on anything that touches it -- a `git stash` round trip,
# the Edit tool saving a file -- producing a whole-file diff on an index
# that is already LF. This file pins line endings explicitly so the
# system-level setting has nothing left to decide.

# Default: let git sniff text vs binary itself (text=auto), and normalise
# whatever it calls text to LF in both the index and the working tree,
# whatever the machine's own core.autocrlf says.
* text=auto eol=lf

# PowerShell scripts. `git ls-files --eol` on develop shows these already
# stored LF, and PowerShell (both powershell.exe and pwsh) runs an
# LF-only .ps1 without issue; pin LF explicitly so nothing flips it later
# and the index never gets a mass rewrite to match a different pin.
*.ps1 text eol=lf

# cmd.exe batch files: none exist in this repo today, but cmd.exe's own
# parser is documented to mishandle LF-only batch files on some Windows
# versions (GOTO labels and multi-line constructs expect a CRLF line
# terminator). Pin CRLF so a .bat/.cmd added later is correct by default
# instead of inheriting the LF default above.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf

# POSIX shell scripts must stay LF: a CRLF shebang line fails with
# "/bin/sh^M: bad interpreter" under WSL, Git Bash and every CI runner
# this repo uses.
*.sh text eol=lf

# uv's lockfile is plain text and already stored LF on develop; pin it
# explicitly like every other text file instead of leaving it to the
# catch-all rule above, since a tool-generated file is the first place a
# future change to the default would be missed.
uv.lock text eol=lf

# Binary formats: never run through the text/eol normalisation machinery,
# whatever the machine's line-ending settings are. None of these are
# tracked in this repo today; the rules are here so one added later does
# not silently get treated as text.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.ico binary
*.webp binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf binary
*.eot binary
*.pdf binary
*.zip binary
